<!-- ===== HEADER ===== -->
<div class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Generative AI Competitive Landscape</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">User Sentiment &amp; Complaint Analysis Across Top 5 AI Apps — Q1 2026</div>
    <div class="meta">41,729 user reviews from the US Google Play Store &bull; Prepared for the Product Strategy Team</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ===== 1. EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ===== -->
<div class="section" id="executive-overview">
  <h2 class="section-title">Executive Overview</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Three strategic takeaways from 41,729 reviews across ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini, Perplexity, and Claude.</p>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <p><span class="number">1.</span><strong>Microsoft Copilot dominates user satisfaction.</strong> It leads on both average star rating (4.24) and mean sentiment polarity (0.487), with the lowest overall complaint rate (9.7%). Its free-tier perception and feature breadth give it a structural advantage in user goodwill.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p><span class="number">2.</span><strong>Pricing is the #1 churn risk for Claude and Perplexity.</strong> Claude (8.0%) and Perplexity (7.5%) have the highest pricing-complaint rates — roughly 3–4× Copilot's rate. Perplexity's pricing reviews average just 1.98 stars, the harshest in the dataset. These users aren't confused; they're angry.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p><span class="number">3.</span><strong>Sentiment-rating mismatch is a hidden data quality issue.</strong> 8–14% of reviews show contradictory signals (e.g., 1-star reviews with positive polarity). Perplexity has the highest low-star-positive mismatch rate (11.2%), suggesting users who appreciate the product but rage over pricing or limits. Star ratings alone miss this nuance.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card copilot">
      <div class="app-name">Copilot</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">4.24</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Avg Star Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card chatgpt">
      <div class="app-name">ChatGPT</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">3.81</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Avg Star Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card claude">
      <div class="app-name">Claude</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">3.79</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Avg Star Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card gemini">
      <div class="app-name">Gemini</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">3.51</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Avg Star Rating</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card perplexity">
      <div class="app-name">Perplexity</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">3.45</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Avg Star Rating</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Data Note:</strong> The source dataset is documented as 50,000 reviews (10,000 per app). After parsing with BOM-aware encoding, 41,729 reviews were successfully loaded (ChatGPT: 8,309; Claude: 8,339; Gemini: 8,294; Copilot: 8,389; Perplexity: 8,398). All KPIs in this report are computed from the 41,729 parsed rows. Per-app comparisons remain valid as the sample sizes are closely balanced (~8,300 each).
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== 2. CROSS-APP SENTIMENT COMPARISON ===== -->
<div class="section" id="app-sentiment-comparison">
  <h2 class="section-title">Cross-App Sentiment Comparison</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Comparing star ratings, VADER sentiment polarity, and rating distributions to identify satisfaction leaders and laggards.</p>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Average Star Rating by App</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="chart-star-avg"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Mean Sentiment Polarity by App</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="chart-sentiment-avg"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Star Rating Distribution by App</h3>
    <div class="chart-wrap-wide">
      <canvas id="chart-star-dist" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Comparison Table</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>App</th><th class="num">Avg Star Rating</th><th class="num">Mean Polarity</th><th class="num">% 5-Star</th><th class="num">% 1-Star</th><th class="num">Reviews</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong></td><td class="num">4.24</td><td class="num">+0.487</td><td class="num">68.2%</td><td class="num">11.3%</td><td class="num">8,389</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>ChatGPT</strong></td><td class="num">3.81</td><td class="num">+0.370</td><td class="num">57.1%</td><td class="num">20.8%</td><td class="num">8,309</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Claude</strong></td><td class="num">3.79</td><td class="num">+0.375</td><td class="num">56.3%</td><td class="num">19.7%</td><td class="num">8,339</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Google Gemini</strong></td><td class="num">3.51</td><td class="num">+0.294</td><td class="num">50.0%</td><td class="num">27.2%</td><td class="num">8,294</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Perplexity</strong></td><td class="num">3.45</td><td class="num">+0.313</td><td class="num">52.7%</td><td class="num">31.3%</td><td class="num">8,398</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="data-note">Sentiment polarity is the VADER compound score (−1.0 to +1.0). 10.2% of reviews have 0.0 polarity — often caused by Unicode-styled text that VADER cannot parse, not neutral sentiment.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== 3. THEMATIC COMPLAINT ANALYSIS ===== -->
<div class="section" id="thematic-complaint-analysis">
  <h2 class="section-title">Thematic Complaint Analysis by Application</h2>
